a:
A function that remembers variables from the scope where it was created, even after that scope has finished.
Use cases: data privacy (counter with private count), function factories, debounce/throttle (they keep the timer id in a closure), React hooks' stale closures.
function counter(){ let n=0; return () => ++n; }

Debounce: run only after the user STOPS for X ms (search input, resize end).
Throttle: run at most once every X ms (scroll, mousemove).
function debounce(fn, ms){ let t; return function(...args){ clearTimeout(t); t = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), ms); }; }

async functions always return a Promise; await pauses THAT function (not the thread) until the promise settles. Errors → try/catch.
Sequential: await a(); await b();  (slow if independent)
Parallel: const [x, y] = await Promise.all([a(), b()]);

Type parameters that let one function/type work with many types while keeping type safety.
function first<T>(arr: T[]): T | undefined { return arr[0]; }
type ApiResponse<T> = { data: T; error?: string };
Constraint: function getId<T extends { id: string }>(x: T) { return x.id; }
